Regression tests for GNU Go.

Each .tst file is a test suite: GTP commands that set up positions,
followed by "#?" lines with the expected answers. The game records the
suites load are in games/ (and golois/). See the Texinfo manual,
chapter "Regression", for the file format.

Running the tests

  ctest --test-dir ../build -j8          (add -C Release for Visual Studio)

runs the main suites, one CTest test each. To run suites directly:

  python run_tests.py ../build/src/interface/gnugo owl.tst
  python run_tests.py ../build/src/interface/gnugo --key -j8 --baseline
  python run_tests.py ../build/src/interface/gnugo --all -j8

"python run_tests.py --help" lists the options. The engine may also be
the WebAssembly build (gnugo.js), which is run under node.

Files

  run_tests.py    the test runner (Python 3 only)
  baseline.json   pass / known-fail counts of the main suites; CTest and
                  --baseline fail if a suite does worse. Update it with
                  --key --update-baseline after an improvement.
  tools/          match.py (strength match between two engines),
                  compare_engines.py (do two builds play the same
                  moves?) and wasm_smoke.cjs (WebAssembly module test)
  benchmark/      GTP game logs against other programs

The original shell, awk, Perl and Pike regression tools (regress.sh,
eval.sh, regress.pike, view.pike, ...) are in ../contrib/regress.

Measuring playing strength

Passing more test cases does not mean playing better, so engine changes
should also be checked with games. tools/match.py plays an engine under
test against a reference engine over GTP, alternating colours, and prints
the win rate with a 95% interval and the Elo difference:

  python tools/match.py ../build/src/interface/gnugo REF-GNUGO -n 200 -j8
  python tools/match.py A B --size 19 --level 8 --sgf-dir games-out
  python tools/match.py A B --args-a "--monte-carlo" --json result.json

The natural reference is the unmodified GNU Go 3.8 engine, which is
commit 2910f75 ("modernize init") of this repository:

  git worktree add ../gnugo-3.8 2910f75
  cmake -S ../gnugo-3.8 -B ../gnugo-3.8/build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
  cmake --build ../gnugo-3.8/build --config Release

GNU Go is nearly deterministic, so each pair of games starts from a few
random moves (--opening, default 4), played once with each colour
assignment. When A and B play the same moves from that opening, the pair
splits 1-1 whatever their strength, so the interval is computed over
pairs and the summary reports how many pairs were decisive: that, not
the game count, is the real sample size. (3.8.1 against 3.8 on 9x9:
3 decisive pairs out of 100, +3 Elo, 95% CI -10 to +18.) A 9x9 game takes
about a second at level 10, a 19x19 game about a minute and a half. Telling a 50 Elo
difference apart needs several hundred games.
